<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saad ibn Abi Waqqas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 595–674 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saad ibn Abi Waqqas was an early companion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and a leading commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. He led the Arab army that defeated the Sasanian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a>, opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to Muslim conquest. Accounts of the Rashidun Caliphate present the victory as a decisive moment in the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saad ibn Abi Waqqas was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> into the Banu Zuhrah clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, to which Muhammad's mother Aminah also belonged. He was the seventh free adult man to embrace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, at the age of seventeen according to most reports, though Ibn Abd al-Barr gives nineteen. His mother opposed the conversion and threatened a hunger strike until he left Islam; he did not yield, and she finally gave way. Ibn Ishaq deems a wound that Saad gave a Meccan polytheist with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> bone the first blood shed in Islam. He migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and took part in nearly all the military operations against the Quraysh until the Expedition of Tabuk, including Badr, Uhud and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-mecca">Conquest of Mecca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 636 the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> sent Saad to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to assist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a>, after Abu Ubayd al-Thaqafi had been killed at the Battle of the Bridge. Saad gathered about 30,000 soldiers from the forces already in Iraq. He led the army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a>. Muslim scholars regard the victory as the beginning of the caliphate's permanent hold on Iraq. He then marched on the Sasanian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> army took, and later sent most of the spoils to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>Saad founded the garrison city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, adjacent to al-Hirah, and governed it under Umar. He had a canal and a congregational mosque built there. After complaints about his conduct, Umar dismissed him, saying that he still trusted him but needed to prevent further scandals. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, Saad led a neutral faction that included Ibn Abbas, Abdullah ibn Umar and Anas ibn Malik, and refused appeals from the warring parties; some resented its inactivity, and his son Umar ibn Sa'd criticised it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Saad outlived all the other nine of the ten to whom Paradise was promised, and died at about the age of eighty, around 674. Sunni Muslims honour him as one of the ten and as a companion who fought at Badr. Fifteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a> attributed to him are in Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim. A tradition in which he asks Muhammad how much of his wealth to leave to charity underlies the rule limiting bequests to one third of an estate.</p><p>Chinese Hui Muslim tradition credits Saad with bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Scholars dispute this: Donald Leslie calls it highly unlikely that envoys went to China in Muhammad's lifetime, and many Arab historians reject it for lack of records. Tombs and mosques attributed to him remain part of Chinese Muslims' cultural heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the east, the Battle of al-Qadisiyyah in 636 or 637 CE, where <b>Saad ibn Abi Waqqas</b> defeated the larger army of the Sasanian general Rostam Farrokhzad after several days of fighting, opened Iraq to conquest and was followed by the fall of the Sasanian capital Ctesiphon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=646" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saad ibn Abi Waqqas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=646">What was here in 646 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
